TD.add("devops-cloud", [

{t:"DevOps", d:"A culture and set of practices that shorten the path from writing code to running it reliably in production.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","practice"],
 b:["DevOps grew out of the dysfunction of separating the people who write software from the people who run it: developers optimise for change, operations for stability, and the wall between them produces slow releases and blame after incidents. The fix is shared ownership — you build it, you run it.",
    "In practice it manifests as automation of build, test and deployment; infrastructure defined as code; monitoring that developers actually look at; and blameless incident review. The DORA metrics — deployment frequency, lead time, change failure rate and time to restore — are the standard way to measure whether it is working."],
 k:["Shared ownership of building and operating, not a job title.",
    "DORA metrics: deploy frequency, lead time, change failure rate, restore time.",
    "Automation is the mechanism; culture is the actual change.",
    "Blameless postmortems make failures a source of learning rather than fear."],
 dg:"cicd",
 r:["CI/CD","Infrastructure as Code","Observability","Site Reliability Engineering"]},

{t:"CI/CD", a:"Continuous Integration / Continuous Delivery", d:"Automating the pipeline that builds, tests and releases every change.", l:"core", g:["practice","automation"],
 b:["Continuous integration means every commit is merged to the mainline frequently and automatically built and tested, so integration problems surface within minutes instead of at the end of a release cycle. Continuous delivery means every passing build is deployable at any moment. Continuous deployment goes further and releases automatically.",
    "The value comes from small batches: a change of twenty lines that fails is trivially diagnosable, while a change of two thousand lines that fails could be anything. Pipeline speed matters more than pipeline thoroughness — a suite nobody waits for gets bypassed."],
 k:["CI: merge and verify often. CD: always be deployable.",
    "Small, frequent changes are dramatically easier to diagnose.",
    "Keep the pipeline under ten minutes or people will route around it.",
    "A red main branch is an emergency, not a backlog item."],
 dg:"cicd",
 r:["Continuous Integration","Continuous Deployment","DevOps","Pipeline","Trunk-Based Development"]},

{t:"Continuous Integration", a:"CI", d:"Merging every developer's work into a shared mainline frequently, with automated verification on each merge.", l:"core", g:["practice","automation"],
 b:["The practice is to integrate at least daily and to run the build and test suite on every change. Long-lived branches accumulate divergence until merging becomes a project of its own; frequent integration keeps each merge small enough to be uninteresting.",
    "A CI pipeline should build, run unit and integration tests, lint, type-check and scan dependencies. Anything flaky must be fixed or removed — a suite that fails randomly trains everyone to ignore failures, which defeats the entire purpose."],
 k:["Integrate to mainline at least daily; long branches are the problem CI solves.",
    "Every change builds, tests, lints and scans automatically.",
    "Flaky tests are worse than no tests — they destroy trust in the signal.",
    "Cache dependencies and parallelise to keep it fast."],
 dg:"cicd",
 r:["CI/CD","Trunk-Based Development","Unit Test","Continuous Deployment"]},

{t:"Continuous Deployment", a:"CD", d:"Automatically releasing every change that passes the pipeline, with no manual approval step.", l:"intermediate", g:["practice","automation"],
 b:["Continuous delivery makes every build deployable; continuous deployment actually deploys it. That requires real confidence in the test suite, plus progressive rollout, feature flags, monitoring and a fast automatic rollback — the safety net has to be strong enough that a bad change is caught and reverted in minutes.",
    "The payoff is that changes reach users in minutes rather than weeks, and each deployment is small enough that when something breaks the cause is obvious."],
 k:["No manual gate — the pipeline is the approval.",
    "Requires progressive rollout, monitoring and automated rollback.",
    "Feature flags decouple deploying code from releasing behaviour.",
    "Small deployments mean small, diagnosable failures."],
 dg:"cicd",
 r:["CI/CD","Feature Flag","Canary Deployment","Rollback","Continuous Integration"]},

{t:"Pipeline", a:"Build Pipeline", d:"The automated sequence of stages a change passes through on its way to production.", l:"core", g:["automation","tooling"],
 b:["A typical pipeline runs build, unit tests, integration tests, security and dependency scanning, artefact publishing, deployment to staging, smoke tests, and deployment to production. Each stage gates the next, so a failure stops progression.",
    "Design for fast feedback: run the quickest and most likely-to-fail checks first, parallelise independent stages, and cache aggressively. A pipeline that takes forty minutes is a pipeline developers work around."],
 k:["Fail fast — cheapest and most likely failures first.",
    "Parallelise independent stages; cache dependencies between runs.",
    "The artefact built once should be the artefact promoted through environments.",
    "Pipeline definitions belong in the repository, versioned with the code."],
 dg:"cicd",
 r:["CI/CD","Artifact","GitHub Actions","Jenkins","Environment"]},

{t:"Artifact", d:"The versioned, immutable output of a build that gets deployed.", l:"core", g:["build","deployment"],
 b:["An artefact might be a container image, a JAR, a wheel, a binary or a static bundle. The essential discipline is build once, deploy everywhere: the exact artefact tested in staging is the one promoted to production, so environment differences cannot introduce a difference in the code.",
    "Rebuilding per environment defeats this entirely — a rebuild can pick up a different transitive dependency version and produce something you never tested."],
 k:["Build once, promote the same artefact through every environment.",
    "Immutable and versioned — never overwrite a published artefact.",
    "Rebuilding per environment reintroduces the differences you were avoiding.",
    "Store with a checksum and provenance for supply-chain traceability."],
 r:["Pipeline","Container Image","Container Registry","Semantic Versioning"]},

{t:"Container", d:"An isolated process bundled with its dependencies, sharing the host kernel rather than running a full operating system.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","infrastructure"],
 b:["A container packages an application with its libraries, runtime and configuration so it runs identically anywhere. Isolation comes from Linux kernel features — namespaces for visibility and cgroups for resource limits — not from virtualisation, which is why containers start in milliseconds and cost megabytes rather than gigabytes.",
    "That eliminates the works on my machine class of problem, and it is what makes orchestration practical: identical, disposable units that can be scheduled anywhere."],
 k:["Shares the host kernel — no guest OS, so startup is near-instant.",
    "Namespaces isolate visibility; cgroups limit CPU and memory.",
    "The image includes dependencies, so the environment travels with the code.",
    "Weaker isolation than a VM — a kernel exploit crosses the boundary."],
 dg:"docker-vm",
 r:["Docker","Container Image","Kubernetes","Virtual Machine","Process"]},

{t:"Docker", d:"The tooling that made containers usable, covering image building, running and distribution.", l:"core", g:["tool","infrastructure"],
 b:["Docker did not invent containers — it made them accessible with a simple CLI, a layered image format, and a registry for sharing. A Dockerfile describes how to build an image; `docker run` starts a container from it; a registry distributes it.",
    "The practices that matter for production images are multi-stage builds so compilers and dev dependencies do not ship, running as a non-root user, pinning base image versions, and ordering layers so the ones that change least often come first and stay cached."],
 k:["Multi-stage builds keep build tooling out of the runtime image.",
    "Never run as root; create and use an unprivileged user.",
    "Order Dockerfile layers from least to most frequently changing.",
    "Pin base image tags — `latest` makes builds unreproducible."],
 x:{lang:"dockerfile", code:'FROM node:22-slim AS build\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY package*.json ./\nRUN npm ci                      # cached unless dependencies change\nCOPY . .\nRUN npm run build\n\nFROM node:22-slim\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY --from=build /app/dist ./dist\nCOPY --from=build /app/node_modules ./node_modules\nUSER node\nEXPOSE 3000\nCMD ["node", "dist/server.js"]'},
 dg:"docker-vm",
 r:["Container","Container Image","Dockerfile","Kubernetes","Container Registry"]},

{t:"Container Image", d:"A read-only layered template from which containers are created.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","infrastructure"],
 b:["An image is built from stacked filesystem layers, each representing one build instruction. Layers are content-addressed and shared between images, so pulling a new version of an application only downloads the layers that changed — usually just the application code.",
    "Image size matters for pull time, storage cost and attack surface. Distroless and Alpine base images cut both size and the number of packages a scanner will find vulnerabilities in."],
 k:["Layered and content-addressed — unchanged layers are shared and cached.",
    "Smaller images pull faster and expose less attack surface.",
    "Tags are mutable; digests are not — pin digests for reproducibility.",
    "Scan images for known vulnerabilities in CI."],
 r:["Docker","Container","Container Registry","Dockerfile","Artifact"]},

{t:"Dockerfile", d:"A text file of instructions describing how to build a container image.", l:"core", g:["tool","build"],
 b:["Each instruction — `FROM`, `RUN`, `COPY`, `ENV`, `CMD` — creates a layer. Because layers are cached, instruction order directly determines build speed: copy dependency manifests and install dependencies before copying application source, so a code change does not invalidate the dependency layer.",
    "A `.dockerignore` file is important and frequently forgotten: without it, `COPY . .` pulls in `node_modules`, `.git` and local environment files, bloating the image and potentially leaking secrets."],
 k:["Each instruction is a cached layer — order determines rebuild speed.",
    "Install dependencies before copying source code.",
    "Always add `.dockerignore` — it prevents bloat and secret leakage.",
    "`CMD` can be overridden at runtime; `ENTRYPOINT` is fixed."],
 r:["Docker","Container Image","Container","Build Tool"]},

{t:"Container Registry", d:"A repository that stores and distributes container images.", l:"core", g:["infrastructure","tooling"],
 b:["Docker Hub, GitHub Container Registry, Amazon ECR and Google Artifact Registry store images and serve them to build and deployment systems. Images are pushed with a tag and pulled by tag or by immutable digest.",
    "Production practice: use a private registry for your own images, scan on push, sign images so deployments can verify provenance, and configure retention so old images do not accumulate indefinitely at cost."],
 k:["Push by tag; pull by digest for reproducibility.",
    "Scan on push and block deployment of images with critical findings.",
    "Sign images (Cosign, Notary) and verify signatures at deploy time.",
    "Set retention policies — untagged images accumulate quietly."],
 r:["Container Image","Docker","Artifact","Supply Chain Attack"]},

{t:"Virtual Machine", a:"VM", d:"An emulated computer running its own complete operating system on top of a hypervisor.", l:"core", g:["infrastructure","fundamentals"],
 b:["A hypervisor partitions physical hardware and presents each VM with virtual CPUs, memory, disks and network interfaces. Each VM runs a full guest OS, giving strong isolation — a compromise in one VM does not reach another, because they do not share a kernel.",
    "The cost is weight: gigabytes of disk and seconds to boot, versus megabytes and milliseconds for a container. VMs remain the right choice when you need a different OS, strong multi-tenant isolation, or to run containers on top of them, which is what every managed Kubernetes service does."],
 k:["Full guest OS per VM — strong isolation, heavy footprint.",
    "Type 1 hypervisors run on bare metal; type 2 run on a host OS.",
    "Boot in seconds and consume gigabytes, unlike containers.",
    "Containers usually run inside VMs in cloud environments."],
 dg:"docker-vm",
 r:["Container","Docker","IaaS, PaaS and SaaS","Kubernetes"]},

{t:"Kubernetes", a:"K8s", d:"An orchestrator that runs containers across a cluster, keeping actual state matched to declared state.", l:"core", g:["tool","infrastructure"],
 b:["You declare what you want — three replicas of this image with these resources — and the control plane's reconciliation loop continuously works to make reality match. A pod dies, the loop notices and schedules a replacement. A node fails, its workloads are rescheduled elsewhere.",
    "That declarative model, plus service discovery, rolling updates, secret management, autoscaling and a large ecosystem, is why it became the standard. Its complexity is real and frequently underestimated — for a handful of services, a managed platform is usually the better choice."],
 k:["Declarative: you state the desired state, the loop reconciles.",
    "Pods are ephemeral; Services provide a stable address in front of them.",
    "Always set resource requests and limits, or scheduling misbehaves.",
    "Genuinely complex — do not adopt it for three services."],
 x:{lang:"yaml", code:'apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata: { name: api }\nspec:\n  replicas: 3\n  selector: { matchLabels: { app: api } }\n  template:\n    metadata: { labels: { app: api } }\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: api\n          image: registry.example.com/api@sha256:abc123\n          resources:\n            requests: { cpu: 100m, memory: 128Mi }\n            limits:   { cpu: 500m, memory: 512Mi }\n          readinessProbe:\n            httpGet: { path: /healthz/ready, port: 3000 }'},
 dg:"kubernetes",
 r:["Pod","Container","Docker","Helm","Service Mesh","Autoscaling"]},

{t:"Pod", d:"The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes — one or more containers sharing a network namespace and storage.", l:"intermediate", g:["kubernetes","infrastructure"],
 b:["Containers in a pod share an IP address and can talk over localhost, and they are always scheduled together on the same node. Most pods contain one application container; sidecars add logging agents, service mesh proxies or configuration reloaders.",
    "Pods are deliberately ephemeral. They are created, killed and replaced constantly, and their IP changes each time, which is why you never address a pod directly — a Service provides the stable endpoint."],
 k:["One or more containers sharing network and storage, scheduled together.",
    "Ephemeral by design — never rely on a pod's identity or IP.",
    "Sidecars add cross-cutting capability without changing the app.",
    "Managed by a controller (Deployment, StatefulSet), rarely created directly."],
 dg:"kubernetes",
 r:["Kubernetes","Container","Health Check","Service Mesh"]},

{t:"Helm", d:"A package manager for Kubernetes that templates and versions sets of manifests.", l:"intermediate", g:["tool","kubernetes"],
 b:["A Helm chart bundles the manifests for an application with a `values.yaml` of parameters, so the same chart deploys to staging and production with different replica counts, resource limits and hostnames. Charts are versioned and can be published to repositories.",
    "The criticism is that Go templating over YAML is awkward and error-prone at scale. Kustomize offers an overlay-based alternative with no templating, and both are widely used."],
 k:["Charts template manifests; `values.yaml` parameterises per environment.",
    "Versioned releases with `helm rollback` for reverting.",
    "Templating YAML with Go templates is genuinely awkward.",
    "Kustomize is the templating-free alternative."],
 r:["Kubernetes","Infrastructure as Code","GitOps","YAML"]},

{t:"Infrastructure as Code", a:"IaC", d:"Defining servers, networks and cloud resources in version-controlled files rather than configuring them by hand.", l:"core", g:["practice","automation"],
 b:["Clicking through a cloud console produces infrastructure nobody can reproduce, review or audit. IaC puts the definition in files, so changes go through pull request, environments can be recreated identically, and drift between what is declared and what exists is detectable.",
    "Declarative tools (Terraform, CloudFormation, Pulumi) describe the target state and compute the changes needed. Imperative tools (scripts) describe the steps. Declarative is strongly preferred because it converges to the same state regardless of the starting point."],
 k:["Reviewable, versioned, reproducible — infrastructure gets the same rigour as code.",
    "Declarative tools converge to a target state; scripts do not.",
    "Manual console changes cause drift — detect and reconcile it.",
    "State files contain secrets: store them remotely with encryption and locking."],
 r:["Terraform","GitOps","Configuration Management","Immutable Infrastructure"]},

{t:"Terraform", d:"A declarative infrastructure-as-code tool that provisions resources across many cloud providers.", l:"core", g:["tool","infrastructure"],
 b:["You describe resources in HCL, Terraform compares that against a state file recording what it previously created, and produces a plan of changes. Reviewing the plan before applying is the core safety mechanism — it shows exactly what will be created, changed or destroyed.",
    "The state file is critical infrastructure in its own right: it must live in remote storage with locking, because two engineers applying simultaneously against local state will corrupt it. Watch for changes marked as replacement — that means destroy and recreate, which for a database is catastrophic."],
 k:["Always read the plan; watch for anything marked forces replacement.",
    "Remote state with locking is mandatory for any team.",
    "State contains secrets in plain text — encrypt it at rest.",
    "Modules make configurations reusable across environments."],
 x:{lang:"hcl", code:'resource "aws_db_instance" "main" {\n  identifier        = "app-${var.environment}"\n  engine            = "postgres"\n  instance_class    = var.db_instance_class\n  allocated_storage = 100\n\n  backup_retention_period = 7\n  deletion_protection     = var.environment == "production"\n\n  lifecycle {\n    prevent_destroy = true\n  }\n}'},
 r:["Infrastructure as Code","GitOps","Immutable Infrastructure","Configuration Management"]},

{t:"Ansible", d:"An agentless configuration management tool that runs tasks over SSH to bring servers to a desired state.", l:"intermediate", g:["tool","automation"],
 b:["Ansible playbooks list tasks in YAML, executed over SSH with no agent installed on targets. Modules are designed to be idempotent — installing a package that is already present does nothing — so running a playbook repeatedly converges rather than duplicating work.",
    "It suits configuring existing servers, orchestrating deployments and running ad-hoc operational tasks. Terraform provisions infrastructure; Ansible configures what runs on it, and many teams use both."],
 k:["Agentless — needs only SSH and Python on the target.",
    "Modules are idempotent, so playbooks converge on repeated runs.",
    "Terraform creates resources; Ansible configures them.",
    "Ansible Vault encrypts secrets stored alongside playbooks."],
 r:["Configuration Management","Terraform","Infrastructure as Code","Immutable Infrastructure"]},

{t:"Configuration Management", d:"Keeping systems in a defined, consistent state and controlling how that state changes.", l:"intermediate", g:["practice","operations"],
 b:["Configuration management covers both configuring servers (Ansible, Chef, Puppet) and managing application configuration across environments. The central principle is that configuration lives outside the artefact — the same image runs in staging and production, with behaviour differing only by injected configuration.",
    "Snowflake servers, hand-tuned over years and impossible to reproduce, are what this practice exists to eliminate. Immutable infrastructure takes the idea further by replacing servers rather than modifying them."],
 k:["Configuration is injected at runtime; the artefact stays identical.",
    "Environment variables for simple values, a secret manager for credentials.",
    "Configuration drift means reality no longer matches the definition.",
    "Immutable infrastructure sidesteps drift by replacing rather than patching."],
 r:["Ansible","Infrastructure as Code","Immutable Infrastructure","Twelve-Factor App","Secrets Management"]},

{t:"Immutable Infrastructure", d:"Never modifying running servers — replacing them with new instances built from a fresh image instead.", l:"intermediate", g:["practice","architecture"],
 b:["Under this model, a deployment or a patch means building a new image and launching new instances, then terminating the old ones. Nothing is ever updated in place, which means configuration drift cannot accumulate and every running instance is provably identical to its image.",
    "Rollback becomes trivial — relaunch the previous image. Debugging changes character: you cannot SSH in and fix something, so logging and metrics must be good enough to diagnose from outside."],
 k:["Replace, never patch — drift becomes impossible.",
    "Rollback is relaunching the previous known-good image.",
    "Requires solid observability, since you cannot log in and poke around.",
    "Containers and auto-scaling groups make this the natural default."],
 r:["Container","Infrastructure as Code","Blue-Green Deployment","Configuration Management"]},

{t:"GitOps", d:"Using a git repository as the single source of truth for infrastructure and deployment state.", l:"intermediate", g:["practice","automation"],
 b:["The desired state of a cluster lives in git. An agent running inside the cluster — Argo CD or Flux — continuously compares actual state against the repository and reconciles differences. Deploying means merging a pull request; rolling back means reverting a commit.",
    "The benefits are a complete audit trail, review before any change, automatic drift correction, and no CI system needing production credentials, because the pull happens from inside the cluster rather than being pushed from outside."],
 k:["Git is the source of truth; an in-cluster agent reconciles toward it.",
    "Every change is reviewed, audited and revertible by construction.",
    "Drift is corrected automatically and continuously.",
    "CI never needs cluster credentials — the cluster pulls."],
 r:["Infrastructure as Code","Kubernetes","CI/CD","Git","Immutable Infrastructure"]},

{t:"Blue-Green Deployment", d:"Running two identical production environments and switching traffic between them to release.", l:"intermediate", g:["deployment","pattern"],
 b:["Blue serves live traffic while green receives the new version. Once green is verified, the load balancer or DNS switches traffic to it. Rollback is switching back, which takes seconds and requires no rebuild.",
    "The cost is running double capacity during the transition. The complication is state: database schema changes must be compatible with both versions simultaneously, which is what the expand-and-contract migration pattern exists to handle."],
 k:["Two full environments; release is a traffic switch.",
    "Rollback is instant — switch back.",
    "Requires double capacity during the cutover.",
    "Database schemas must be compatible with both versions at once."],
 r:["Canary Deployment","Rolling Deployment","Rollback","Database Migration"]},

{t:"Canary Deployment", d:"Releasing a new version to a small fraction of traffic first, then expanding if metrics stay healthy.", l:"intermediate", g:["deployment","pattern"],
 b:["Route 1% of traffic to the new version, watch error rates and latency, then go to 10%, 50%, 100%. If metrics degrade at any stage, route back. The blast radius of a bad release is limited to the fraction currently exposed.",
    "It requires good metrics, a defined set of criteria to promote or abort, and ideally automation to make that decision. Manual canaries tend to be promoted by someone glancing at a dashboard, which is better than nothing but not much."],
 k:["Progressive exposure limits the blast radius of a bad release.",
    "Define promotion and abort criteria before starting.",
    "Automate the decision where possible — humans promote optimistically.",
    "Compare canary metrics against the baseline, not against history."],
 r:["Blue-Green Deployment","Rolling Deployment","Feature Flag","Monitoring","A/B Testing"]},

{t:"Rolling Deployment", d:"Replacing instances with the new version a few at a time, keeping the service available throughout.", l:"intermediate", g:["deployment","pattern"],
 b:["A rolling update takes down a small number of old instances, brings up new ones, waits for them to pass readiness checks, and repeats. It is the Kubernetes default and needs no extra capacity beyond a small surge.",
    "During the rollout both versions serve traffic simultaneously, so they must be compatible — API changes, message formats and database schemas all need to work across both. Rollback is slower than blue-green because it is another rolling update in reverse."],
 k:["Gradual replacement with no extra environment required.",
    "Both versions run simultaneously — they must be compatible.",
    "Readiness probes gate progression to the next batch.",
    "Rollback is a second rolling update, so it is not instant."],
 r:["Blue-Green Deployment","Canary Deployment","Kubernetes","Health Check"]},

{t:"Rollback", d:"Reverting to the previous working version after a bad deployment.", l:"core", g:["deployment","operations"],
 b:["The first response to a production incident caused by a deployment should be to restore service, and rolling back is almost always faster than diagnosing and fixing forward. That requires rollback to be a rehearsed, single-command operation rather than an improvisation.",
    "The complication is irreversible changes: a database migration that dropped a column, a message consumed from a queue, an email already sent. This is why migrations are designed to be backward compatible and applied separately from code deploys."],
 k:["Restore service first, diagnose afterwards.",
    "Rollback must be a rehearsed one-step operation.",
    "Destructive database migrations make rollback impossible — sequence them separately.",
    "Keep the previous artefact ready to deploy at all times."],
 x:{lang:"text",code:"SAFE:  add nullable column -> deploy code writing both\n       -> backfill -> switch reads -> drop old column\n       (every step is individually reversible)\n\nUNSAFE: rename the column in one migration\n        (the old code breaks the moment it runs, and\n         rolling back the code does not bring it back)"},
 r:["Blue-Green Deployment","Database Migration","Incident Response","Feature Flag"]},

{t:"Autoscaling", d:"Automatically adjusting the number of running instances in response to load.", l:"core", g:["scaling","cloud"],
 b:["Horizontal autoscaling adds and removes instances based on a metric — CPU, memory, request rate or queue depth. Vertical autoscaling adjusts the resources of existing instances. The goal is meeting demand without paying for permanently provisioned peak capacity.",
    "The tuning problems are consistent: scaling on CPU when the bottleneck is I/O, scaling up faster than new instances can warm up, and flapping when thresholds are too close together. Scale out aggressively and in conservatively, and always set a floor above zero for latency-sensitive services."],
 k:["Scale on the metric that actually reflects load — often queue depth, not CPU.",
    "Scale out fast, scale in slowly, to avoid flapping.",
    "Account for instance startup time in the threshold.",
    "Keep a minimum replica count to avoid cold starts."],
 r:["Horizontal Scaling","Kubernetes","Serverless","Cold Start","Load Balancer"]},

{t:"Observability", d:"The ability to understand a system's internal state from the signals it emits.", l:"core", g:["operations","monitoring"],
 b:["Monitoring answers questions you thought of in advance — is CPU high, is the error rate up. Observability is about answering questions you did not anticipate: why is this specific customer's request slow on Tuesdays. That requires high-cardinality, high-dimensional data rather than pre-aggregated counters.",
    "The three pillars are metrics (cheap aggregate numbers over time), logs (detailed discrete events) and traces (the path of one request across services). Structured logging and consistent trace ids are what let you correlate between them."],
 k:["Metrics, logs and traces — each answers a different kind of question.",
    "Monitoring checks known failure modes; observability explores unknown ones.",
    "High cardinality is the point — pre-aggregation destroys the ability to ask new questions.",
    "Propagate a correlation id through every service and log line."],
 r:["Monitoring","Logging","Distributed Tracing","Metrics","Prometheus"]},

{t:"Monitoring", d:"Collecting and alerting on measurements of a system's health and behaviour.", l:"core", g:["operations","reliability"],
 b:["Monitoring tracks known signals and alerts when they cross thresholds. The four golden signals — latency, traffic, errors and saturation — cover most of what matters for a service, and the RED method (rate, errors, duration) is a simpler variant for request-driven systems.",
    "The discipline is in alerting. Alert on symptoms users experience, not on causes; every alert must be actionable and have a runbook; and anything that pages someone at 3 a.m. must genuinely require a human at 3 a.m. Alert fatigue is how real incidents get missed."],
 k:["Four golden signals: latency, traffic, errors, saturation.",
    "Alert on user-visible symptoms, not on internal causes.",
    "Every page must be actionable and documented.",
    "Alert fatigue causes missed incidents — prune aggressively."],
 r:["Observability","Prometheus","Alerting","SLA, SLO and SLI","Logging"]},

{t:"Logging", d:"Recording discrete events as a system runs, for debugging and audit.", l:"core", g:["operations","observability"],
 b:["Structured logs — JSON with consistent fields — are queryable in a way that free-text messages are not. Every log line should carry a timestamp, level, service name, and a correlation id that ties it to the request it belongs to.",
    "The two recurring failures are logging too little to diagnose anything and logging so much that costs balloon and the signal drowns. And never log secrets, tokens, passwords or personal data — logs are widely readable, retained for a long time, and shipped to third-party services."],
 k:["Structure logs as JSON with consistent field names.",
    "Include a correlation id on every line to reconstruct a request.",
    "Never log credentials, tokens or personal data.",
    "Log volume drives cost — sample high-frequency debug output."],
 r:["Observability","Distributed Tracing","Monitoring","Error Handling"]},

{t:"Metrics", d:"Numeric measurements aggregated over time, used for dashboards and alerts.", l:"core", g:["operations","observability"],
 b:["The common types are counters (monotonically increasing totals), gauges (values that go up and down), and histograms (distributions from which percentiles are computed). Metrics are cheap to store because they aggregate, which is also their limitation — you cannot drill into an individual request.",
    "Always look at percentiles rather than averages. A mean latency of 100 ms is compatible with 1% of users waiting five seconds, and those are the users who complain."],
 k:["Counters, gauges and histograms answer different questions.",
    "Percentiles, not averages — the mean hides the tail entirely.",
    "Cheap because aggregated; cannot explain an individual request.",
    "High-cardinality labels explode storage — bound them deliberately."],
 r:["Monitoring","Prometheus","Observability","Percentile","Latency"]},

{t:"Distributed Tracing", d:"Following a single request as it travels through multiple services, recording timing at each hop.", l:"intermediate", g:["operations","observability"],
 b:["A trace is a tree of spans, each representing one operation, connected by a trace id propagated through every call. It shows exactly where the time went in a request that touched eight services — which is otherwise essentially impossible to determine.",
    "OpenTelemetry has become the vendor-neutral standard for instrumentation. Because tracing every request is expensive, sampling is used — head-based sampling decides at the start, tail-based keeps traces that turned out to be slow or failed, which is far more useful."],
 k:["Trace id propagates through every service; spans record each operation.",
    "The only practical way to find latency in a multi-service request path.",
    "OpenTelemetry is the vendor-neutral instrumentation standard.",
    "Tail-based sampling keeps the traces that actually matter."],
 r:["Observability","Microservices","Logging","Latency","Service Mesh"]},

{t:"Prometheus", d:"An open-source monitoring system that scrapes metrics from targets and stores them as time series.", l:"core", g:["tool","monitoring"],
 b:["Prometheus pulls metrics from HTTP endpoints your services expose, stores them with labels as dimensions, and provides PromQL for querying. The pull model means Prometheus knows immediately when a target is unreachable, which is itself a useful signal.",
    "It is the de facto standard for infrastructure and Kubernetes monitoring. Its main constraint is that it is designed for operational monitoring, not long-term storage — Thanos, Cortex or Mimir provide that. Watch label cardinality: a label containing user ids will exhaust memory."],
 k:["Pull-based scraping from endpoints your service exposes.",
    "PromQL queries multidimensional labelled time series.",
    "High-cardinality labels (user id, request id) will exhaust memory.",
    "Pair with Thanos or Mimir for long-term retention."],
 r:["Metrics","Monitoring","Grafana","Kubernetes","Time-Series Database"]},

{t:"Grafana", d:"A visualisation and dashboarding tool that queries many data sources and displays them together.", l:"core", g:["tool","monitoring"],
 b:["Grafana connects to Prometheus, Loki, Elasticsearch, cloud monitoring services and SQL databases, and renders dashboards combining metrics, logs and traces. It also handles alerting rules and notification routing.",
    "Good dashboards answer specific questions and are ordered by importance. The common failure is a wall of forty panels that nobody reads during an incident because finding the relevant one takes longer than guessing."],
 k:["Queries many sources into one dashboard.",
    "Dashboards should answer specific questions, not display everything available.",
    "Store dashboard definitions as code, not as clicked-together state.",
    "Link dashboards to runbooks so an alert leads somewhere useful."],
 r:["Prometheus","Monitoring","Metrics","Observability","Data Visualisation"]},

{t:"Alerting", d:"Notifying a human when a system requires intervention.", l:"core", g:["operations","reliability"],
 b:["A good alert is urgent, actionable and user-affecting. If it does not need action now, it belongs on a dashboard or in a ticket, not on a pager. Alerts should fire on symptoms — error rate, latency, unavailability — rather than on causes such as high CPU, which is frequently harmless.",
    "Every alert needs a runbook explaining what it means and what to check. Alerts that fire regularly and are routinely ignored are worse than no alert, because they teach on-call engineers that pages can be dismissed."],
 k:["If it does not need action right now, it should not page anyone.",
    "Alert on user-visible symptoms, not internal causes.",
    "Every alert needs a runbook with concrete first steps.",
    "Review and prune alerts regularly — noisy pages get ignored."],
 r:["Monitoring","SLA, SLO and SLI","Incident Response","Error Budget","On-Call"]},

{t:"SLA, SLO and SLI", d:"Three related reliability terms: the contractual promise, the internal target, and the actual measurement.", l:"core", g:["reliability","operations"],
 b:["An SLI is the measurement — the percentage of requests served successfully under 300 ms. An SLO is the internal target for that indicator — 99.9% over 30 days. An SLA is the external contract with financial consequences for breaching it, and is always set looser than the SLO so you have warning before penalties apply.",
    "Choose SLIs that reflect what users actually experience. Server-side uptime is not a user experience; successful, fast responses from the user's perspective is."],
 k:["SLI measures, SLO targets internally, SLA promises contractually.",
    "Set the SLO stricter than the SLA so you have room to react.",
    "99.9% allows about 43 minutes of downtime per month.",
    "Base SLIs on user-visible outcomes, not server-side uptime."],
 r:["Error Budget","Monitoring","Alerting","High Availability"]},

{t:"Error Budget", d:"The amount of unreliability an SLO permits, treated as a resource that can be spent.", l:"intermediate", g:["reliability","practice"],
 b:["A 99.9% availability SLO permits 0.1% failure — roughly 43 minutes a month. That allowance is the error budget. While budget remains, teams can ship aggressively; when it is exhausted, the policy is to stop feature work and spend the time on reliability.",
    "This turns the perennial argument between velocity and stability into a shared, measurable rule. Consistently unused budget is also a signal — it means the SLO is stricter than it needs to be, and you are over-investing in reliability."],
 k:["The permitted unreliability, expressed as a spendable quantity.",
    "Budget exhausted means feature work stops until reliability improves.",
    "Replaces an argument with a rule both sides agreed to in advance.",
    "Never spending the budget means the target is too strict."],
 r:["SLA, SLO and SLI","Monitoring","Incident Response","DevOps"]},

{t:"Incident Response", d:"The process of detecting, mitigating and resolving a production failure.", l:"core", g:["operations","reliability"],
 b:["An effective response has defined roles — an incident commander who coordinates, communications lead, and subject matter experts — a severity scale that sets expectations, and a single channel where everything is recorded. The priority order is always restore service, then find the cause.",
    "Rolling back, failing over or disabling a feature flag are mitigations that restore service in minutes. Diagnosing the root cause while users are affected is the classic mistake that turns a ten-minute incident into a two-hour one."],
 k:["Mitigate first — rollback, failover, feature flag — then investigate.",
    "One incident commander coordinating; everyone else reports to them.",
    "Communicate status to stakeholders on a fixed cadence.",
    "Record a timeline as you go; memory reconstructs it badly."],
 r:["Postmortem","Rollback","Alerting","On-Call","Error Budget"]},

{t:"Postmortem", a:"Incident Review", d:"A written analysis after an incident covering what happened, why, and what will change.", l:"core", g:["practice","reliability"],
 b:["A postmortem records a timeline, the impact, the contributing factors and a set of concrete action items with owners. The essential principle is blamelessness: people act reasonably given the information and incentives they had, so the question is what about the system allowed this, not who did it.",
    "Blame produces hiding, which removes the information that would prevent recurrence. Action items must be specific and tracked — a postmortem whose actions are never completed is a document, not an improvement."],
 k:["Blameless: examine the system that allowed the failure, not the individual.",
    "Timeline, impact, contributing factors, action items with owners.",
    "Multiple contributing causes are the norm; a single root cause is rare.",
    "Untracked action items mean the incident will recur."],
 x:{lang:"text",code:"IMPACT     checkout down 14:02-14:31 (29 min), ~4,000 users\nTRIGGER    deploy #4471\nCAUSE      migration held a lock on `orders` during peak\nWHY NOT\nCAUGHT     staging has 200 rows; production has 40 million\n\nACTIONS\n  [ ] lock timeout on migrations      @ana    12 Sep\n  [ ] production-sized load test      @ravi   19 Sep\n  [ ] deploy freeze during peak hours @team   done"},
 r:["Incident Response","DevOps","Error Budget","Chaos Engineering"]},

{t:"On-Call", d:"A rota of engineers responsible for responding to production alerts outside working hours.", l:"intermediate", g:["operations","practice"],
 b:["On-call makes reliability everyone's concern: engineers who are woken by their own service's alerts have direct motivation to reduce them. Sustainable rotations need enough people that any individual is on call infrequently, a clear escalation path, and runbooks for every alert that pages.",
    "The health metric is pages per shift. A rotation that consistently pages several times a night is not sustainable, and the correct response is to fix the underlying noise rather than to accept it."],
 k:["The people who build a service should be the ones it wakes.",
    "Track pages per shift as a health metric of the rotation.",
    "Every paging alert needs a runbook.",
    "Compensate on-call and enforce recovery time after night pages."],
 r:["Alerting","Incident Response","Monitoring","DevOps"]},

{t:"Chaos Engineering", d:"Deliberately injecting failures into a system to verify it handles them before they happen unplanned.", l:"advanced", g:["practice","reliability"],
 b:["The premise is that you do not know how a system behaves under failure until it fails, so you should cause failures deliberately, under controlled conditions, while you are watching. Terminate instances, inject latency, drop network connections, exhaust disk.",
    "Netflix's Chaos Monkey popularised it. The discipline is to form a hypothesis, limit the blast radius, run the experiment, and either confirm resilience or fix what broke. Running chaos experiments before you have basic observability just creates outages you cannot explain."],
 k:["Form a hypothesis, limit blast radius, run, observe, fix.",
    "Start in staging, then production with tight controls.",
    "Requires solid observability first — otherwise you learn nothing.",
    "Verifies that timeouts, retries and failovers actually work."],
 r:["Incident Response","Observability","Circuit Breaker","High Availability"]},

{t:"High Availability", a:"HA", d:"Designing a system to keep operating despite the failure of individual components.", l:"core", g:["reliability","architecture"],
 b:["High availability means no single point of failure: redundant instances across availability zones, replicated databases with automatic failover, load balancers that route around unhealthy targets, and health checks that detect problems quickly.",
    "Availability targets are expressed in nines, and each additional nine costs disproportionately more. 99.9% permits 43 minutes of downtime a month; 99.99% permits four. Decide which the business actually needs before engineering for it."],
 k:["Eliminate single points of failure at every layer.",
    "Spread across availability zones, not just across instances.",
    "Each additional nine costs far more than the last.",
    "Failover must be tested regularly or it will not work when needed."],
 r:["Load Balancer","Replication","Disaster Recovery","SLA, SLO and SLI","Availability Zone"]},

{t:"Disaster Recovery", a:"DR", d:"The plan and capability to restore service after a major failure such as losing an entire region.", l:"intermediate", g:["reliability","operations"],
 b:["Disaster recovery is defined by two numbers: recovery point objective, how much data you can afford to lose, and recovery time objective, how long restoration may take. Those numbers determine the architecture — continuous replication and a warm standby cost far more than nightly backups.",
    "The plan is worthless untested. Organisations discover during real disasters that backups were incomplete, the restore procedure was undocumented, or the standby region lacked a critical dependency. Run the drill."],
 k:["RPO is acceptable data loss; RTO is acceptable downtime.",
    "The architecture follows from those two numbers.",
    "Untested DR plans fail — run restoration drills on a schedule.",
    "Backups must be tested by restoring, not by confirming they exist."],
 r:["Backup","High Availability","Replication","Incident Response"]},

{t:"IaaS, PaaS and SaaS", d:"Three cloud service models distinguished by how much the provider manages.", l:"core", g:["cloud","fundamentals"],
 b:["Infrastructure as a Service gives you virtual machines, storage and networking — you manage the OS and everything above. Platform as a Service gives you a runtime where you deploy code and the provider handles the OS, scaling and patching. Software as a Service is a finished application you simply use.",
    "The trade is control against operational burden. Containers and serverless sit between IaaS and PaaS, and most organisations use all of these simultaneously rather than choosing one."],
 k:["IaaS: you manage the OS upward. PaaS: you manage the code. SaaS: you manage nothing.",
    "More management by the provider means less control and more lock-in.",
    "Serverless and managed containers sit between IaaS and PaaS.",
    "Most organisations use all three concurrently."],
 r:["Virtual Machine","Serverless","Cloud Region","Container"]},

{t:"Cloud Region", d:"A geographic location containing a cluster of a cloud provider's data centres.", l:"core", g:["cloud","infrastructure"],
 b:["Region choice determines latency to users, which data protection laws apply, what services are available (new features roll out to major regions first) and cost, which varies noticeably between regions. Data transfer between regions is charged and adds latency.",
    "Regions contain multiple availability zones — physically separate data centres with independent power and networking. Spreading across zones within a region is the standard way to survive a data centre failure without cross-region complexity."],
 k:["Choose for latency, data residency law, service availability and cost.",
    "Cross-region data transfer costs money and adds latency.",
    "Regions contain multiple isolated availability zones.",
    "Not every service exists in every region."],
 r:["Availability Zone","High Availability","IaaS, PaaS and SaaS","Latency"]},

{t:"Availability Zone", a:"AZ", d:"An isolated data centre within a cloud region, with independent power, cooling and networking.", l:"intermediate", g:["cloud","infrastructure"],
 b:["Zones within a region are physically separate but connected by low-latency links, so an application can span them with only single-digit milliseconds of added latency. A fire, power failure or flood affecting one zone leaves the others running.",
    "Deploying across at least two zones is the baseline for production, and managed databases offer multi-AZ deployment with automatic failover. This is the cheapest meaningful availability improvement available."],
 k:["Physically isolated failure domains within one region.",
    "Low-latency links make multi-AZ deployment practical.",
    "Two or more zones is the production baseline.",
    "Cross-AZ data transfer is charged on some providers."],
 r:["Cloud Region","High Availability","Load Balancer","Replication"]},

{t:"Virtual Private Cloud", a:"VPC", d:"A logically isolated network within a cloud provider where you control addressing, routing and access.", l:"intermediate", g:["cloud","networking"],
 b:["A VPC gives you a private IP address range divided into subnets, with route tables, security groups and network ACLs controlling traffic. The standard pattern is public subnets holding load balancers and NAT gateways, and private subnets holding application servers and databases with no route from the internet.",
    "Getting this right is foundational security: a database in a public subnet with a permissive security group is one of the most common serious cloud misconfigurations."],
 k:["Public subnets for load balancers; private subnets for applications and data.",
    "Security groups are stateful and instance-level; NACLs are stateless and subnet-level.",
    "Databases must never be reachable from the internet.",
    "VPC endpoints keep traffic to cloud services off the public internet."],
 r:["Cloud Region","Firewall","Subnet","Network Address Translation","Zero Trust"]},

{t:"FinOps", a:"Cloud Cost Management", d:"The practice of making cloud spending visible, attributable and controllable.", l:"intermediate", g:["cloud","practice"],
 b:["Cloud costs grow silently because provisioning is a single API call and nobody deletes anything. FinOps brings engineering, finance and product together around visibility: tag every resource with an owner and purpose, attribute costs to teams, and set budgets with alerts.",
    "The recurring wins are consistent: right-size over-provisioned instances, delete unattached storage and idle load balancers, use committed-use discounts for steady baseline load and spot instances for interruptible work, and set lifecycle policies on object storage."],
 k:["Tag everything with owner and purpose, or costs cannot be attributed.",
    "Idle and orphaned resources are the most common waste.",
    "Reserved and committed-use pricing cuts steady-state cost substantially.",
    "Data transfer, especially cross-region and egress, is a frequent surprise."],
 r:["Autoscaling","Cloud Region","Serverless","Inference Cost"]},

{t:"GitHub Actions", d:"GitHub's built-in CI/CD system, defining workflows as YAML files in the repository.", l:"core", g:["tool","automation"],
 b:["Workflows are triggered by repository events — push, pull request, release, schedule — and run jobs on hosted or self-hosted runners. The marketplace of reusable actions covers most common tasks, which makes getting started quick.",
    "The security considerations matter: pin third-party actions to a commit SHA rather than a tag, because a tag can be moved to malicious code; use OIDC for cloud credentials rather than long-lived secrets; and be careful with workflows triggered by pull requests from forks."],
 k:["YAML workflows triggered by repository events.",
    "Pin third-party actions to a commit SHA, never a mutable tag.",
    "Use OIDC federation instead of storing long-lived cloud keys.",
    "`pull_request_target` runs with write access — a known injection risk."],
 x:{lang:"yaml", code:'name: CI\non: [push, pull_request]\n\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@b4ffde6  # pinned SHA, not @v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@1e60f6\n        with: { node-version: 22, cache: npm }\n      - run: npm ci\n      - run: npm run lint && npm test\n      - run: npm audit --audit-level=high'},
 r:["CI/CD","Pipeline","Git","Supply Chain Attack"]},

{t:"Jenkins", d:"A long-established, self-hosted automation server for building CI/CD pipelines.", l:"intermediate", g:["tool","automation"],
 b:["Jenkins predates the current generation of CI services and remains widely deployed, particularly where builds must run on private infrastructure. Its plugin ecosystem is enormous, covering essentially every tool, and pipelines are defined in a Groovy-based Jenkinsfile stored with the code.",
    "The costs are that you operate it yourself — upgrades, plugin compatibility, controller scaling — and that its plugin surface has historically been a source of vulnerabilities. Keep it patched and behind authentication."],
 k:["Self-hosted, so you control the infrastructure and carry the operations.",
    "Declarative pipelines in a Jenkinsfile, versioned with the code.",
    "Plugin sprawl causes upgrade pain and security exposure.",
    "Never expose a Jenkins controller to the internet unauthenticated."],
 r:["CI/CD","Pipeline","Groovy","GitHub Actions"]},

{t:"Environment", d:"A separate deployment of a system — development, staging, production — used at different stages of delivery.", l:"core", g:["practice","deployment"],
 b:["The usual progression is local development, a shared development environment, staging, and production. The value of staging depends entirely on how closely it resembles production: a staging environment with a tenth of the data and different configuration will not catch the problems that matter.",
    "Environments should differ only by configuration, never by build. Ephemeral preview environments spun up per pull request and destroyed on merge are increasingly the norm and remove the contention over a single shared staging environment."],
 k:["Same artefact everywhere; only configuration differs.",
    "Staging is only useful to the extent it resembles production.",
    "Per-pull-request preview environments remove shared-staging contention.",
    "Never test with real production personal data in lower environments."],
 r:["Configuration Management","Pipeline","Artifact","Twelve-Factor App"]},

{t:"Site Reliability Engineering", a:"SRE", d:"Google's discipline of applying software engineering methods to operations problems.", l:"intermediate", g:["practice","reliability"],
 b:["SRE treats operations as a software problem: automate toil, define reliability numerically with SLOs and error budgets, and cap the proportion of time spent on manual operational work so there is always time to engineer it away.",
    "It overlaps heavily with DevOps but is more prescriptive: explicit error budgets governing release velocity, a defined limit on toil, and blameless postmortems as a formal process. Where DevOps is a culture, SRE is an implementation of it."],
 k:["Automate toil; cap manual operational work as a share of time.",
    "Error budgets connect reliability to release velocity by policy.",
    "100% reliability is the wrong target — it costs more than it returns.",
    "A prescriptive implementation of DevOps principles."],
 r:["DevOps","Error Budget","SLA, SLO and SLI","Postmortem","Observability"]},

{t:"Build Tool", d:"Software that compiles, tests and packages a project reproducibly.", l:"core", g:["tooling","build"],
 b:["Maven and Gradle for the JVM, npm and pnpm for JavaScript, Cargo for Rust, Make for C, and Bazel or Nx for large multi-language repositories. A build tool resolves dependencies, orders compilation, runs tests and produces a distributable artefact.",
    "The properties that matter at scale are incrementality (rebuild only what changed), caching (including shared caches across a team) and hermeticity (the build depends only on declared inputs, so it produces identical output anywhere)."],
 k:["Resolves dependencies, orders work, produces the artefact.",
    "Incremental builds and shared caches dominate developer experience at scale.",
    "Hermetic builds depend only on declared inputs and are reproducible.",
    "Commit the lock file — it is what makes builds repeatable."],
 r:["Package Manager","Artifact","CI/CD","Pipeline"]},

{t:"npm", a:"Node Package Manager", d:"The default package manager and registry for JavaScript.", l:"core", g:["tooling","javascript"],
 b:["npm resolves dependencies from `package.json`, writes exact resolved versions to `package-lock.json`, and installs into `node_modules`. The registry is the largest package repository in existence, with the transitive dependency depth that implies — a small project can easily pull in a thousand packages.",
    "Use `npm ci` in CI rather than `npm install`: it installs exactly what the lock file specifies and fails if the two disagree, which is what reproducibility requires. pnpm and Yarn are faster alternatives that use the same registry."],
 k:["`npm ci` in CI; `npm install` only when changing dependencies.",
    "Commit `package-lock.json` — it is the reproducibility guarantee.",
    "Deep transitive trees mean you trust far more code than you chose.",
    "Run `npm audit` and review post-install scripts."],
 r:["Package Manager","Node.js","Supply Chain Attack","Semantic Versioning","Build Tool"]},
{t:"Cloud Computing", d:"Renting computing resources on demand over a network, billed by usage rather than owned outright.", l:"core", g:["fundamentals","cloud"],
 b:["Before the cloud, capacity was a guess made months in advance: you bought servers, waited for delivery, racked them, and owned them whether or not anyone used your product. Being wrong in either direction was expensive, which is why starting anything was slow.",
    "The cloud replaced that with an API call billed by the second. The three layers usually named are **IaaS** (you rent a machine and manage the operating system), **PaaS** (you deploy code and the platform manages the machine) and **SaaS** (you use somebody else's application). The trade is that capital expenditure becomes operating expenditure, and the bill becomes a live engineering concern rather than a fixed cost — which is why cost awareness is now part of an engineer's job."],
 k:["Capacity on demand, billed by usage, provisioned by an API call.",
    "IaaS, PaaS and SaaS differ in how much of the stack somebody else runs.",
    "Cheapest for spiky, occasional or growing workloads; owned hardware wins at steady full utilisation.",
    "The bill is a live engineering signal, not a fixed cost — an unwatched cloud account is an unbounded one."],
 r:["Serverless","Container","Autoscaling","Cloud Region","Infrastructure as Code","Virtual Private Cloud"]},

{t:"Durability", d:"The probability that stored data survives over time, as distinct from whether it is reachable right now.", l:"core", g:["storage","reliability"],
 b:["Durability and availability answer different questions. Durability asks *is the data still there*; availability asks *can I read it at this moment*. A storage system can be highly durable and temporarily unavailable — the bytes are safe, the service is down — and that distinction decides which failure you are designing against.",
    "Object stores quote figures like eleven nines (99.999999999%) of annual durability, achieved by writing every object to several devices across separate failure domains and continuously verifying checksums. The number is a statement about hardware failure only. It does not protect against a wrong delete, a bad deployment overwriting a file, or ransomware — which is why versioning, object lock and tested restores exist alongside it."],
 k:["Durability is *will it survive*; availability is *can I read it now*.",
    "High durability comes from redundancy across independent failure domains plus checksum verification.",
    "Eleven nines says nothing about your own mistakes — versioning and object lock cover those.",
    "A backup nobody has restored is a hypothesis, not a backup."],
 r:["Object Storage","Replication","Backup","Availability Zone","Encryption"]},

{t:"Configuration Drift", d:"The gap that opens between what infrastructure is declared to be and what it actually is.", l:"intermediate", g:["infrastructure","practice"],
 b:["Drift appears the moment somebody fixes something by hand. A security group edited in the console during an incident, a package installed on one server, a setting changed and never written down — each is individually reasonable and collectively fatal, because the environment can no longer be recreated and staging stops resembling production.",
    "Infrastructure as code makes drift *visible* rather than preventing it: the next plan proposes undoing your manual change, which is the tool telling you the two pictures disagree. The durable fixes are to make the declared configuration the only path to change, to detect drift on a schedule, and to prefer immutable infrastructure — replace the server rather than adjusting it — so there is nothing for drift to accumulate in."],
 k:["Drift starts with a reasonable manual fix and ends with an environment nobody can recreate.",
    "Infrastructure as code surfaces drift as an unexpected diff in the next plan.",
    "Detect it on a schedule; a drift found six months later is an archaeology project.",
    "Immutable infrastructure removes the surface entirely — replace, do not adjust."],
 r:["Infrastructure as Code","Terraform","Immutable Infrastructure","Idempotency","Configuration Management"]},

{t:"Content-Addressable Storage", a:"CAS", d:"Storing data under a key derived from its own contents, usually a cryptographic hash.", l:"intermediate", g:["storage","architecture"],
 b:["Instead of naming a file and hoping the name is unique and stable, you hash the bytes and use the hash as the address. Identical content therefore lands in exactly one place, which makes deduplication automatic, writes idempotent, and integrity checkable — re-hashing the data verifies it has not changed.",
    "Git is the best-known example: every blob, tree and commit is addressed by its own hash, which is why history cannot be silently rewritten. Container registries, backup systems and document-ingestion pipelines use the same idea. In an AI pipeline it is what makes re-ingesting a corpus cheap: the chunks whose hashes are unchanged do not need re-embedding, so cost scales with edits rather than with corpus size."],
 k:["The address is derived from the content, so identical content stores once.",
    "Deduplication, idempotent writes and integrity verification come for free.",
    "Git, container registries and backup systems are all built on it.",
    "In an ingestion pipeline it turns re-processing cost from corpus size into edit size."],
 r:["Hashing","Idempotency","Object Storage","Git","Container Image"]}

]);
